    <!-- ── 04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact ──────────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
        <h2>Resource containment is not exact-action approval</h2>
        <p>Microsoft documents rejection of ungranted access in Enforcement and Learning modes. Permissive mode records but allows it. This is resource containment, not demonstrated payload binding, reviewer authority verification, or single-use authorization.<sup><a href="#s3">3</a></sup></p>
        <p>The Decision Authority Layer is the runtime enforcement layer within Decision Governance. Enterprise trigger logic identifies consequential actions requiring an authority decision. System access does not establish authority to execute a specific action.</p>
        <p>DAL-X architecture describes signed tokens bound to the exact payload, current reviewer authority checks, single-use consumption, and downstream rejection.<sup><a href="#s5">5</a></sup> Conditional assessment: these controls could stop an unapproved form submission or secret-bearing outbound request only if the action triggers review, the reviewer has valid authority, and every relevant execution path rejects missing or mismatched authority. A valid approval does not establish that the content is correct.</p>

        <p class="weekly-status">DAL-X runs in a simulated enterprise environment. Production enforcement controls are still being completed. No live enterprise deployment is established.<sup><a href="#s6">6</a></sup></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch ──────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch</div>
        <h2>Test the submission and the outbound request</h2>
        <p>For form submission, test direct browser and API paths without approval. Effective control requires rejection before external state changes, with a rejection receipt and unchanged destination state.</p>
        <p>For file transmission, approve one destination and payload, then alter either and replay the authorization. Require rejection and evidence that no bytes reached the destination across helper tools, retries, and alternate network paths.</p>
        <p>Watch for Anthropic's containment coverage evidence and any Copilot remediation. Resolve the conflicting notification dates: police report 7 October, while Anthropic says 8 October. Neither source establishes exact-action enforcement across every path.</p>

        <div class="weekly-question">Can your enterprise prove that an agent cannot submit a real form or transmit local secrets without valid authority for that exact action?</div>

